1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,601
She was handed a number and told to bide her time.

2
00:00:02,901 --> 00:00:03,923
Forty-seven.

3
00:00:04,223 --> 00:00:06,220
Two hours later, she got up and left.

4
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:13,416
The case is Correa v. Hospital San Francisco, decided by the United States Court of

5
00:00:13,416 --> 00:00:17,772
Appeals for the First Circuit on the thirty-first of October, 1995.

6
00:00:18,072 --> 00:00:21,416
Nobody at that hospital ever told Carmen Gonzalez no.

7
00:00:21,716 --> 00:00:23,202
Nobody discharged her.

8
00:00:23,502 --> 00:00:28,135
A jury awarded her family seven hundred thousand dollars, and the First Circuit did

9
00:00:28,135 --> 00:00:29,586
not reduce it by a dollar.

10
00:00:31,386 --> 00:00:35,861
On the morning of the sixth of September, 1991, a sixty-five-year-old widow in

11
00:00:35,861 --> 00:00:39,188
Puerto Rico woke up feeling, in her son's words, real bad.

12
00:00:39,488 --> 00:00:40,417
Chills.

13
00:00:40,717 --> 00:00:41,785
Cold sweat.

14
00:00:42,085 --> 00:00:43,060
Dizziness.

15
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:44,521
Chest pains.

16
00:00:44,821 --> 00:00:47,468
Her name was Carmen Gloria Gonzalez Figueroa.

17
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:52,665
She asked her son Angel to drive her to the emergency room at Hospital San Francisco

18
00:00:52,665 --> 00:00:55,988
— HSF in the opinion — where she had been treated before.

19
00:00:56,288 --> 00:00:59,539
She was inside no later than one o'clock in the afternoon.

20
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:04,501
Start with what was in dispute, because almost everything in this case runs through

21
00:01:04,501 --> 00:01:04,669
it.

22
00:01:04,969 --> 00:01:09,353
The evidence is conflicted, the court wrote, as to whom she saw and what that person

23
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:10,867
was told about her condition.

24
00:01:11,167 --> 00:01:15,715
Angel Correa testified that he implored the receptionist to have someone take care

25
00:01:15,715 --> 00:01:18,876
of my mother, because she feels sick and has chest pains.

26
00:01:19,176 --> 00:01:23,542
The Hospital disagrees, maintaining that its personnel were told only that Ms.

27
00:01:23,542 --> 00:01:25,445
Gonzalez felt dizzy and nauseated.

28
00:01:25,745 --> 00:01:26,813
Two accounts.

29
00:01:27,113 --> 00:01:28,042
One counter.

30
00:01:28,342 --> 00:01:30,432
No way, now, to stand between them.

31
00:01:30,732 --> 00:01:33,797
What follows is therefore not a set of appellate findings.

32
00:01:34,097 --> 00:01:38,781
When the losing side attacks the sufficiency of the evidence, the court of appeals

33
00:01:38,781 --> 00:01:43,522
must, in the opinion's own words, take both the facts and the reasonable inferences

34
00:01:43,522 --> 00:01:47,007
therefrom in the light most hospitable to the jury's verdict.

35
00:01:47,307 --> 00:01:51,923
Judge Selya, writing for the panel, described what he was working with in one

36
00:01:51,923 --> 00:01:56,659
phrase: the thicket of conflicting testimony and the chasmal gaps in the direct

37
00:01:56,659 --> 00:01:57,199
evidence.

38
00:01:57,499 --> 00:02:01,586
So this is the version of that afternoon a jury was entitled to believe.

39
00:02:01,886 --> 00:02:03,697
It is not the only one offered.

40
00:02:03,997 --> 00:02:09,072
In any event — and this the opinion states as undisputed — a Hospital employee

41
00:02:09,072 --> 00:02:14,407
assigned the patient a number, forty-seven, told her to bide her time, and checked

42
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:16,164
her medical insurance card.

43
00:02:16,464 --> 00:02:20,086
That card matters, though not for the reason it looks like it should.

44
00:02:20,386 --> 00:02:25,245
Her health plan required her to seek routine treatment at Hospmed, a local clinic,

45
00:02:25,245 --> 00:02:29,808
during its business hours, but allowed her to see any appropriate health-care

46
00:02:29,808 --> 00:02:31,764
provider in case of an emergency.

47
00:02:32,064 --> 00:02:34,340
She was entitled to be where she was.

48
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:35,662
Then the waiting.

49
00:02:35,962 --> 00:02:38,609
After approximately one hour, Angel called Esther.

50
00:02:38,909 --> 00:02:40,024
Esther came.

51
00:02:40,324 --> 00:02:43,668
Angel left, and heard number twenty-four called on his way out.

52
00:02:43,968 --> 00:02:48,315
Now accompanied by her daughter, the opinion says, Ms. Gonzalez maintained her

53
00:02:48,315 --> 00:02:52,327
unproductive vigil for an additional forty-five to seventy-five minutes.

54
00:02:52,627 --> 00:02:55,553
The Hospital staff continued blithely to ignore her.

55
00:02:55,853 --> 00:02:56,782
Blithely.

56
00:02:57,082 --> 00:03:01,680
That is the court's adverb, not ours, and it is the only description of the staff's

57
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:03,398
conduct anywhere in the record.

58
00:03:03,698 --> 00:03:04,766
Add up the clock.

59
00:03:05,066 --> 00:03:09,457
From no later than one o'clock until somewhere between three and half past three,

60
00:03:09,457 --> 00:03:13,957
she was inside that emergency room — something between two hours and two and a half

61
00:03:13,957 --> 00:03:17,698
— with a number in her hand and a card that had already been checked.

62
00:03:17,998 --> 00:03:19,391
No physician saw her.

63
00:03:19,691 --> 00:03:24,474
Weary of waiting, the two women drove to the office of Dr. Acacia Rojas Davis, the

64
00:03:24,474 --> 00:03:28,907
director of Hospmed, arriving there between three and half past three in the

65
00:03:28,907 --> 00:03:29,490
afternoon.

66
00:03:29,790 --> 00:03:32,901
The opinion does not describe anyone turning her away.

67
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:35,895
It does not describe anyone discharging her.

68
00:03:36,195 --> 00:03:38,563
She left because she had been waiting.

69
00:03:38,863 --> 00:03:41,789
Can a hospital break a federal law by doing nothing at all?

70
00:03:42,089 --> 00:03:46,836
At the clinic she told Dr. Rojas that she was nauseated and had taken a double dose

71
00:03:46,836 --> 00:03:49,009
of her high blood pressure medication.

72
00:03:49,309 --> 00:03:52,467
Her blood pressure was very low: ninety over sixty.

73
00:03:52,767 --> 00:03:57,136
When she began vomiting, the physician immediately started intravenous infusions of

74
00:03:57,136 --> 00:03:57,504
fluids.

75
00:03:57,804 --> 00:04:00,451
She also dispensed medicine to control the emesis.

76
00:04:00,751 --> 00:04:05,721
Despite these ministrations, the opinion continues, Ms. Gonzalez's condition

77
00:04:05,721 --> 00:04:07,160
steadily deteriorated.

78
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:10,479
Dr. Rojas had to resuscitate her soon after her arrival.

79
00:04:10,779 --> 00:04:15,064
The doctor then attempted to transfer her to the Hato Rey Community Hospital, but

80
00:04:15,064 --> 00:04:16,863
could not commandeer an ambulance.

81
00:04:17,163 --> 00:04:21,874
As Dr. Rojas began preparations to transport Ms. Gonzalez by van, the patient

82
00:04:21,874 --> 00:04:22,364
expired.

83
00:04:22,664 --> 00:04:27,377
Her death, which occurred at around half past four, was attributed to hypovolemic

84
00:04:27,377 --> 00:04:27,726
shock.

85
00:04:28,026 --> 00:04:31,323
That is the whole of what the record says about how she died.

86
00:04:31,623 --> 00:04:34,781
The opinion never says the hospital's delay killed her.

87
00:04:35,081 --> 00:04:37,264
It never says she would have lived.

88
00:04:37,564 --> 00:04:42,639
Her death was attributed to hypovolemic shock, it happened at another facility, and

89
00:04:42,639 --> 00:04:44,902
it happened under a physician's care.

90
00:04:45,202 --> 00:04:48,221
The federal case that follows is not about her death.

91
00:04:48,521 --> 00:04:51,586
It is about what happened while she was still sitting there.

92
00:04:52,186 --> 00:04:54,090
One more thing from that afternoon.

93
00:04:54,390 --> 00:04:58,670
Dr. Rojas testified that a nurse called from HSF to advise her that the patient

94
00:04:58,670 --> 00:05:00,892
would be coming to Hospmed for treatment.

95
00:05:01,192 --> 00:05:06,499
She said that this conversation probably occurred earlier that day, perhaps around

96
00:05:06,499 --> 00:05:07,276
one o'clock.

97
00:05:07,576 --> 00:05:12,018
The panel called that a datum suggesting that HSF tried to shunt Ms. Gonzalez to

98
00:05:12,018 --> 00:05:14,960
Hospmed as soon as it scrutinized her insurance card.

99
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:16,235
Probably.

100
00:05:16,535 --> 00:05:17,464
Perhaps.

101
00:05:17,764 --> 00:05:18,879
Suggesting.

102
00:05:19,179 --> 00:05:24,380
Nobody ever proved why the call was made, or by whom, or what was in anybody's mind.

103
00:05:24,680 --> 00:05:29,377
What the jury had was a physician's recollection of a call, placed at about the hour

104
00:05:29,377 --> 00:05:30,439
a card was checked.

105
00:05:32,239 --> 00:05:33,493
Seven people sued.

106
00:05:33,793 --> 00:05:38,976
Ms. Gonzalez's three adult children — Angel, Esther and Gloria — and four of her

107
00:05:38,976 --> 00:05:44,288
grandchildren, the progeny of her late son, Felix Correa, who had predeceased her.

108
00:05:44,588 --> 00:05:49,464
The grandchildren are named in the opinion: Glendalis, Glorimar, Angelis and Sarai.

109
00:05:49,764 --> 00:05:53,433
They sued in the United States District Court for the District of Puerto Rico.

110
00:05:53,733 --> 00:05:58,527
They alleged two violations of a federal statute called EMTALA — inappropriate

111
00:05:58,527 --> 00:06:03,567
screening and improper transfer — and a pendent claim of medical malpractice under

112
00:06:03,567 --> 00:06:04,182
local law.

113
00:06:04,482 --> 00:06:06,850
The malpractice claim did not survive.

114
00:06:07,150 --> 00:06:10,587
The district court dismissed it, and that ruling was never appealed.

115
00:06:10,887 --> 00:06:15,349
From that point on, nobody in this case was arguing about whether she was treated

116
00:06:15,349 --> 00:06:15,624
well.

117
00:06:15,924 --> 00:06:17,921
Only about whether she was looked at.

118
00:06:18,221 --> 00:06:23,411
The case went to the jury on the two EMTALA theories, and the jury returned a series

119
00:06:23,411 --> 00:06:25,141
of special written findings.

120
00:06:25,441 --> 00:06:30,185
It found that she did present an emergency medical condition, and the evidence to

121
00:06:30,185 --> 00:06:33,289
that effect, the appeals court later said, was ample.

122
00:06:33,589 --> 00:06:37,583
It found that the hospital had failed to afford her an appropriate screening.

123
00:06:37,883 --> 00:06:43,276
And it found, separately, that the hospital had violated the statute by transferring

124
00:06:43,276 --> 00:06:46,614
her improperly, before her condition had stabilized.

125
00:06:46,914 --> 00:06:49,097
That third finding needs a marker on it.

126
00:06:49,397 --> 00:06:51,440
The court of appeals never reviewed it.

127
00:06:51,740 --> 00:06:56,650
In footnote seven the panel wrote that because it upheld the screening finding, it

128
00:06:56,650 --> 00:07:01,680
need not comment upon the jury's finding that HSF also violated EMTALA by improperly

129
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:05,393
transferring Ms. Gonzalez before her condition had stabilized.

130
00:07:05,693 --> 00:07:08,247
It plays no part in what this case decided.

131
00:07:08,547 --> 00:07:09,522
Then the money.

132
00:07:09,822 --> 00:07:14,392
The jury assessed two hundred thousand dollars in damages on the decedent's account,

133
00:07:14,392 --> 00:07:15,534
payable to the heirs.

134
00:07:15,834 --> 00:07:20,270
And five hundred thousand dollars for the pain, suffering, and mental anguish

135
00:07:20,270 --> 00:07:24,994
experienced by the survivors — one hundred thousand apiece for the three children,

136
00:07:24,994 --> 00:07:28,048
and fifty thousand apiece for the four grandchildren.

137
00:07:28,348 --> 00:07:30,298
Seven hundred thousand dollars in total.

138
00:07:30,598 --> 00:07:35,879
The district court denied the Hospital's post-trial motions for judgment as a matter

139
00:07:35,879 --> 00:07:38,771
of law, a new trial, and remission of damages.

140
00:07:39,071 --> 00:07:44,226
Judge Juan Perez-Gimenez, who had tried the case, gave the award what the appeals

141
00:07:44,226 --> 00:07:47,662
court later called his unconditional seal of approval.

142
00:07:47,962 --> 00:07:49,402
So the hospital appealed.

143
00:07:49,702 --> 00:07:51,142
Comprehensively.

144
00:07:51,442 --> 00:07:56,614
HSF assigns error in no fewer than eight iterations, the opinion notes: five attacks

145
00:07:56,614 --> 00:08:01,663
on the sufficiency of the evidence, one demand for a new trial, and two attacks on

146
00:08:01,663 --> 00:08:02,402
the damages.

147
00:08:02,702 --> 00:08:04,606
Eight ways of saying the same thing.

148
00:08:04,906 --> 00:08:06,067
None of them worked.

149
00:08:06,367 --> 00:08:09,850
Why they failed depends on what this statute actually promises.

150
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:12,379
It is less than most people assume.

151
00:08:12,679 --> 00:08:16,394
In 1995, the First Circuit had never interpreted EMTALA.

152
00:08:16,694 --> 00:08:22,007
The opening line of the opinion says as much: this appeal requires us to interpret,

153
00:08:22,007 --> 00:08:26,679
for the first time, the Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act.

154
00:08:26,979 --> 00:08:31,885
Six years earlier a plaintiff had put an EMTALA question to the same court, in a

155
00:08:31,885 --> 00:08:36,852
case called Wilson v. Atlanticare Medical Center, and the court refused to answer

156
00:08:36,852 --> 00:08:39,611
it, because the issue had not been preserved.

157
00:08:39,911 --> 00:08:41,861
So the panel started where Congress did.

158
00:08:42,161 --> 00:08:46,821
As health-care costs spiralled upward and third-party payments assumed increased

159
00:08:46,821 --> 00:08:51,423
importance, Congress became concerned — this is the House report, quoted in the

160
00:08:51,423 --> 00:08:56,199
opinion — about the increasing number of reports that hospital emergency rooms are

161
00:08:56,199 --> 00:09:00,975
refusing to accept or treat patients with emergency conditions if the patient does

162
00:09:00,975 --> 00:09:02,548
not have medical insurance.

163
00:09:02,848 --> 00:09:05,820
The enforcement mechanism was not a police force.

164
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:07,095
It was money.

165
00:09:07,395 --> 00:09:11,967
Needing a carrot to make health-care providers more receptive to the stick, the

166
00:09:11,967 --> 00:09:16,712
court wrote, Congress simultaneously amended the Social Security Act, conditioning

167
00:09:16,712 --> 00:09:21,342
hospitals' continued participation in the federal Medicare program — a lucrative

168
00:09:21,342 --> 00:09:25,972
source of institutional revenue — on acceptance of the duties imposed by the new

169
00:09:25,972 --> 00:09:26,203
law.

170
00:09:26,503 --> 00:09:30,172
A hospital that wants Medicare money accepts EMTALA's duties.

171
00:09:30,472 --> 00:09:32,655
That is the entire architecture.

172
00:09:32,955 --> 00:09:34,209
Nobody is commanded.

173
00:09:34,509 --> 00:09:35,902
Everybody is paid.

174
00:09:36,202 --> 00:09:38,060
So what did that money buy the patient?

175
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:43,252
For patients such as Ms. Gonzalez, the court said, the statute has two linchpin

176
00:09:43,252 --> 00:09:43,933
provisions.

177
00:09:44,233 --> 00:09:48,760
First: a participating hospital must afford an appropriate medical screening to all

178
00:09:48,760 --> 00:09:52,360
persons who come to its emergency room seeking medical assistance.

179
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:57,267
Second: if an emergency medical condition exists, the participating hospital must

180
00:09:57,267 --> 00:10:01,987
render the services that are necessary to stabilize the patient's condition, unless

181
00:10:01,987 --> 00:10:06,423
transferring the patient to another facility is medically indicated and can be

182
00:10:06,423 --> 00:10:08,357
accomplished with relative safety.

183
00:10:08,657 --> 00:10:12,720
To add bite to its provisions, EMTALA establishes monetary penalties for

184
00:10:12,720 --> 00:10:17,404
noncompliance — not more than fifty thousand dollars for each negligent violation —

185
00:10:17,404 --> 00:10:22,032
and authorizes private rights of action against those who transgress its mandates.

186
00:10:22,332 --> 00:10:24,004
Three elements make a claim.

187
00:10:24,304 --> 00:10:30,219
One: the hospital is a participating hospital, covered by EMTALA, that operates an

188
00:10:30,219 --> 00:10:31,734
emergency department.

189
00:10:32,034 --> 00:10:35,238
Two: the patient arrived at the facility seeking treatment.

190
00:10:35,538 --> 00:10:40,291
Three: the hospital either did not afford the patient an appropriate screening in

191
00:10:40,291 --> 00:10:45,044
order to determine if she had an emergency medical condition, or bade farewell to

192
00:10:45,044 --> 00:10:49,503
the patient — whether by turning her away, discharging her, or improvidently

193
00:10:49,503 --> 00:10:54,021
transferring her — without first stabilizing the emergency medical condition.

194
00:10:54,321 --> 00:10:56,132
Element three has two doors.

195
00:10:56,432 --> 00:10:58,475
This case went through the first one.

196
00:10:58,775 --> 00:11:03,001
And then the panel decided something that was genuinely open at the time.

197
00:11:03,301 --> 00:11:07,726
The hospital argued that a screening claim requires proof that the patient actually

198
00:11:07,726 --> 00:11:10,499
had an emergency medical condition when she arrived.

199
00:11:10,799 --> 00:11:12,889
Some courts had said so in dictum.

200
00:11:13,189 --> 00:11:18,431
The First Circuit refused: this suggestion finds no purchase in the statute's text,

201
00:11:18,431 --> 00:11:19,505
and we reject it.

202
00:11:19,805 --> 00:11:24,695
The failure appropriately to screen, by itself, is sufficient to ground liability as

203
00:11:24,695 --> 00:11:28,071
long as the other elements of the cause of action are met.

204
00:11:28,371 --> 00:11:31,808
The duty to look does not depend on what the looking would have found.

205
00:11:32,108 --> 00:11:34,987
The court fenced that in immediately, in the same footnote.

206
00:11:35,287 --> 00:11:39,420
Civil monetary penalties are imposable irrespective of resulting harm.

207
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:44,641
But the statutory damage remedy requires a showing of personal harm as a direct

208
00:11:44,641 --> 00:11:47,568
result of a participating hospital's violation.

209
00:11:47,868 --> 00:11:52,525
It is difficult, the panel said, to imagine a case in which a patient who does not

210
00:11:52,525 --> 00:11:56,614
present an emergency medical condition will meet the statute's causation

211
00:11:56,614 --> 00:11:57,295
requirement.

212
00:11:57,595 --> 00:11:59,220
Here the question never arose.

213
00:11:59,520 --> 00:12:04,128
The plaintiffs alleged she presented an emergency medical condition, the jury so

214
00:12:04,128 --> 00:12:06,950
found, and the evidence to that effect was ample.

215
00:12:07,250 --> 00:12:10,501
So the statute applied — if the hospital did.

216
00:12:12,301 --> 00:12:16,666
The hospital's first argument on appeal was that EMTALA did not cover it at all.

217
00:12:16,966 --> 00:12:18,731
Not that it had complied.

218
00:12:19,031 --> 00:12:20,889
That the law did not reach it.

219
00:12:21,189 --> 00:12:25,503
The plaintiffs, it said, had failed to prove the predicate fact: that HSF had

220
00:12:25,503 --> 00:12:29,873
accepted the federal government's carrot and agreed to come under the statute.

221
00:12:30,173 --> 00:12:34,576
At trial the plaintiffs had introduced into evidence, without objection, HSF's

222
00:12:34,576 --> 00:12:38,867
policy statement outlining for its employees and associates how the Hospital

223
00:12:38,867 --> 00:12:42,480
intended to ensure compliance with EMTALA in its emergency room.

224
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:47,046
Then, during the defense case, the hospital's own health services administrator

225
00:12:47,046 --> 00:12:51,420
testified that he had dutifully instructed his staff regarding the fine points of

226
00:12:51,420 --> 00:12:52,393
EMTALA compliance.

227
00:12:52,693 --> 00:12:55,433
It had written a manual about complying with EMTALA.

228
00:12:55,733 --> 00:12:58,798
Its executive told a jury he had trained the staff on it.

229
00:12:59,098 --> 00:13:01,838
And then it argued that EMTALA did not apply.

230
00:13:02,138 --> 00:13:04,135
The court's reply runs eight words.

231
00:13:04,435 --> 00:13:07,314
This argument has the shrill ring of desperation.

232
00:13:07,614 --> 00:13:12,640
On appeal it added that the policy statement was inadmissible hearsay and that no

233
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:14,626
proper foundation had been laid.

234
00:13:14,926 --> 00:13:17,202
Neither objection had been made at trial.

235
00:13:17,502 --> 00:13:21,914
Voiced for the first time on appeal, absent plain error, they were waived.

236
00:13:22,214 --> 00:13:26,818
The jury, the court held, had a rational basis on which to conclude that HSF is

237
00:13:26,818 --> 00:13:30,898
among the ninety-nine percent of American hospitals covered by EMTALA.

238
00:13:31,198 --> 00:13:36,057
That figure is the court's, offered without a citation, and it is worth leaving

239
00:13:36,057 --> 00:13:38,025
exactly where the court left it.

240
00:13:38,325 --> 00:13:40,183
Ninety-nine is not one hundred.

241
00:13:40,483 --> 00:13:41,923
Then the second argument.

242
00:13:42,223 --> 00:13:46,640
The Hospital asserts that it had no obligation to screen because Ms. Gonzalez did

243
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:50,675
not have an emergency medical condition when she reported to its facility.

244
00:13:50,975 --> 00:13:53,994
This theory of defense, the panel wrote, is doubly flawed.

245
00:13:54,294 --> 00:13:56,430
The first flaw had already been dealt with.

246
00:13:56,730 --> 00:14:01,060
The duty runs to all who enter the hospitals' emergency departments, whether or not

247
00:14:01,060 --> 00:14:04,346
they are in the throes of a medical emergency when they arrive.

248
00:14:04,646 --> 00:14:06,596
The second flaw was evidentiary.

249
00:14:06,896 --> 00:14:11,212
Angel testified that he told the receptionist his mother was experiencing chest

250
00:14:11,212 --> 00:14:11,540
pains.

251
00:14:11,840 --> 00:14:16,499
And HSF concedes, the opinion records, that a patient of Ms. Gonzalez's age who

252
00:14:16,499 --> 00:14:20,863
suffered from chest pains would be regarded as having an emergency medical

253
00:14:20,863 --> 00:14:21,453
condition.

254
00:14:21,753 --> 00:14:26,623
The hospital's answer was to point at Dr. Rojas, who had testified that Ms. Gonzalez

255
00:14:26,623 --> 00:14:30,855
did not develop chest pains until some time after she arrived at Hospmed.

256
00:14:31,155 --> 00:14:33,245
Believe the physician, it said.

257
00:14:33,545 --> 00:14:34,567
Not the son.

258
00:14:34,867 --> 00:14:39,505
There is no principled way in which we can accommodate HSF's request, the court

259
00:14:39,505 --> 00:14:39,975
replied.

260
00:14:40,275 --> 00:14:44,687
Credibility choices are generally for the jury, not for the court of appeals.

261
00:14:44,987 --> 00:14:46,287
Then it went further.

262
00:14:46,587 --> 00:14:51,504
Even taking the doctor at her word, the chest pains might well have spurted and

263
00:14:51,504 --> 00:14:52,438
later subsided.

264
00:14:52,738 --> 00:14:57,304
And even if Ms. Gonzalez had complained only of nausea and dizziness, that

265
00:14:57,304 --> 00:15:01,932
symptomatology — as Dr. Rojas explained — might well herald the onset of an

266
00:15:01,932 --> 00:15:06,066
emergency medical condition in the case of a hypertensive diabetic.

267
00:15:06,366 --> 00:15:07,945
Which Carmen Gonzalez was.

268
00:15:08,245 --> 00:15:10,706
Everything now turns on one adjective.

269
00:15:11,006 --> 00:15:15,331
EMTALA requires an appropriate medical screening, but does not explain what

270
00:15:15,331 --> 00:15:16,254
constitutes one.

271
00:15:16,554 --> 00:15:20,409
The adjectival phrase, the court observed, is not self-defining.

272
00:15:20,709 --> 00:15:25,139
Then it quoted the Sixth Circuit, and for one paragraph the opinion stops sounding

273
00:15:25,139 --> 00:15:26,003
like a judgment.

274
00:15:26,303 --> 00:15:31,029
Appropriate is one of the most wonderful weasel words in the dictionary, and a great

275
00:15:31,029 --> 00:15:35,080
aid to the resolution of disputed issues in the drafting of legislation.

276
00:15:35,380 --> 00:15:39,649
Who, after all, can be found to stand up for inappropriate treatment or actions of

277
00:15:39,649 --> 00:15:40,117
any sort?

278
00:15:40,417 --> 00:15:45,153
Selya then added his own flourish, borrowed from Emerson: appropriateness, like

279
00:15:45,153 --> 00:15:48,869
nature, is a mutable cloud which is always and never the same.

280
00:15:49,169 --> 00:15:50,794
A cloud is not a standard.

281
00:15:51,094 --> 00:15:54,902
So the courts had built one, and the First Circuit adopted it.

282
00:15:55,202 --> 00:15:59,884
A hospital fulfills its statutory duty to screen patients in its emergency room if

283
00:15:59,884 --> 00:16:04,566
it provides for a screening examination reasonably calculated to identify critical

284
00:16:04,566 --> 00:16:09,191
medical conditions that may be afflicting symptomatic patients, and provides that

285
00:16:09,191 --> 00:16:13,474
level of screening uniformly to all those who present substantially similar

286
00:16:13,474 --> 00:16:14,103
complaints.

287
00:16:14,403 --> 00:16:15,703
Then the compression.

288
00:16:16,003 --> 00:16:20,290
The essence of this requirement is that there be some screening procedure, and that

289
00:16:20,290 --> 00:16:21,994
it be administered even-handedly.

290
00:16:22,294 --> 00:16:23,780
Some procedure.

291
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:25,195
Even-handed.

292
00:16:25,495 --> 00:16:28,189
That is the whole of the federal duty.

293
00:16:28,489 --> 00:16:30,207
And immediately, the limit.

294
00:16:30,507 --> 00:16:34,176
EMTALA does not create a cause of action for medical malpractice.

295
00:16:34,476 --> 00:16:38,670
A refusal to follow regular screening procedures in a particular instance

296
00:16:38,670 --> 00:16:40,049
contravenes the statute.

297
00:16:40,349 --> 00:16:44,866
But faulty screening, in a particular case, as opposed to disparate screening or

298
00:16:44,866 --> 00:16:48,197
refusing to screen at all, does not contravene the statute.

299
00:16:48,497 --> 00:16:52,026
A hospital that examines you badly has not violated this law.

300
00:16:52,326 --> 00:16:56,831
A hospital that examines you differently, or does not examine you at all, has.

301
00:16:57,131 --> 00:16:59,174
Which raises a measurement problem.

302
00:16:59,474 --> 00:17:00,914
Differently from what?

303
00:17:01,214 --> 00:17:03,257
Uniform compared to which standard?

304
00:17:03,557 --> 00:17:07,040
The court's answer, in this case, was to use the hospital's own.

305
00:17:07,340 --> 00:17:11,982
Its rules, as explicated in its policy statement, required its emergency room

306
00:17:11,982 --> 00:17:16,503
personnel promptly to take the vital signs of every patient who visited the

307
00:17:16,503 --> 00:17:17,046
facility.

308
00:17:17,346 --> 00:17:19,807
To make a written record of all such visits.

309
00:17:20,107 --> 00:17:23,404
To treat patients suffering from chest pains as critical cases.

310
00:17:23,704 --> 00:17:27,884
And to refer all critical cases to an in-house physician immediately.

311
00:17:28,184 --> 00:17:29,391
Four rules.

312
00:17:29,691 --> 00:17:31,874
The hospital wrote them for itself.

313
00:17:32,174 --> 00:17:34,589
Now set them beside what the trial produced.

314
00:17:34,889 --> 00:17:39,571
From the evidence adduced at trial, the court wrote — especially Angel Correa's

315
00:17:39,571 --> 00:17:43,898
recollections, and the Hospital's utter inability to produce any records.

316
00:17:44,198 --> 00:17:46,381
The hospital could not produce a record.

317
00:17:46,681 --> 00:17:47,703
Not a thin one.

318
00:17:48,003 --> 00:17:48,885
None at all.

319
00:17:49,185 --> 00:17:53,725
A hospital whose own rules demanded a written record of every visit came to trial

320
00:17:53,725 --> 00:17:57,312
with nothing for a patient who had been inside it for two hours.

321
00:17:57,612 --> 00:18:02,511
From that, the jury reasonably could have inferred that the Hospital did not measure

322
00:18:02,511 --> 00:18:07,002
up to the parameters it had established, and that the decedent was denied the

323
00:18:07,002 --> 00:18:11,668
screening — monitoring of vital signs, compilation of a written chart, immediate

324
00:18:11,668 --> 00:18:16,100
referral to an in-house physician — that HSF customarily afforded to persons

325
00:18:16,100 --> 00:18:17,674
complaining of chest pains.

326
00:18:17,974 --> 00:18:21,736
The conclusion is one sentence and every clause of it is a limit.

327
00:18:22,036 --> 00:18:27,019
In this case, HSF's delay in attending to the patient was so egregious and lacking

328
00:18:27,019 --> 00:18:31,881
in justification as to amount to an effective denial of a screening examination.

329
00:18:32,181 --> 00:18:33,203
In this case.

330
00:18:33,503 --> 00:18:34,757
So egregious.

331
00:18:35,057 --> 00:18:36,682
Lacking in justification.

332
00:18:36,982 --> 00:18:41,807
And then the door the panel deliberately left shut: thus, we need not decide whether

333
00:18:41,807 --> 00:18:46,460
mere negligence in failing to expedite screening would itself violate the federal

334
00:18:46,460 --> 00:18:46,920
statute.

335
00:18:47,520 --> 00:18:49,749
That ends the matter, the opinion says.

336
00:18:51,549 --> 00:18:53,546
The hospital had one more argument.

337
00:18:53,846 --> 00:18:56,400
Put at its strongest, its case went like this.

338
00:18:56,700 --> 00:18:59,254
Begin with what the court said about its own record.

339
00:18:59,554 --> 00:19:03,269
To be sure, the evidence in this case is not particularly precise.

340
00:19:03,569 --> 00:19:08,027
But facts at trial, as in life, do not always appear in black and white.

341
00:19:08,327 --> 00:19:12,414
Juries and judges frequently must distinguish between manifold shades of gray.

342
00:19:12,714 --> 00:19:15,268
The panel found that the grays predominate here.

343
00:19:15,568 --> 00:19:20,127
Nobody had established who met her at that counter, or what they were told when she

344
00:19:20,127 --> 00:19:20,676
got there.

345
00:19:20,976 --> 00:19:25,434
The hospital's own witness put the chest pains after she reached the clinic.

346
00:19:25,734 --> 00:19:30,228
The malpractice claim, the one that asks whether her care was competent, had been

347
00:19:30,228 --> 00:19:32,003
dismissed and was not on appeal.

348
00:19:32,303 --> 00:19:36,668
Even the panel allowed that an emergency room cannot serve everyone at once.

349
00:19:36,968 --> 00:19:39,290
Put like that, where is the violation?

350
00:19:39,590 --> 00:19:41,076
Nobody sent her away.

351
00:19:41,376 --> 00:19:42,955
Nobody discharged her.

352
00:19:43,255 --> 00:19:46,041
She was given a number, and she left before it was called.

353
00:19:46,341 --> 00:19:50,540
The hospital said it had neither denied Ms. Gonzalez an initial screening nor

354
00:19:50,540 --> 00:19:52,285
refused her essential treatment.

355
00:19:52,585 --> 00:19:57,013
Its point was that it gave the patient a number, and would have ministered to her

356
00:19:57,013 --> 00:19:57,833
had she waited.

357
00:19:58,133 --> 00:20:00,176
The court's answer is four words.

358
00:20:00,476 --> 00:20:02,334
This contention is spurious.

359
00:20:02,634 --> 00:20:04,213
Then two reasons, in order.

360
00:20:04,513 --> 00:20:06,278
The first is the telephone call.

361
00:20:06,578 --> 00:20:10,572
According to Dr. Rojas, HSF referred Ms. Gonzalez to Hospmed.

362
00:20:10,872 --> 00:20:16,621
And the panel adds a dry aside of its own — we note, as an aside, that HSF called

363
00:20:16,621 --> 00:20:18,395
Dr. Rojas as its witness.

364
00:20:18,695 --> 00:20:23,302
The hospital's own witness described a call she said came from the hospital, about a

365
00:20:23,302 --> 00:20:25,661
patient who would be coming to her instead.

366
00:20:25,961 --> 00:20:30,818
If the jury believed the physician's testimony, the court wrote, it could well have

367
00:20:30,818 --> 00:20:35,558
found that HSF never intended to treat the decedent, or, at the least, was itself

368
00:20:35,558 --> 00:20:37,664
responsible for truncating her wait.

369
00:20:37,964 --> 00:20:41,772
If the jury believed that, the hospital was the reason she got up and drove there.

370
00:20:42,072 --> 00:20:45,927
Then the wait it says she abandoned would be a wait it had already ended.

371
00:20:46,227 --> 00:20:49,571
The second reason does not depend on the telephone call at all.

372
00:20:49,871 --> 00:20:54,562
Regardless of motive, the court wrote, a complete failure to attend a patient who

373
00:20:54,562 --> 00:20:59,311
presents a condition that practically everyone knows may indicate an immediate and

374
00:20:59,311 --> 00:21:03,944
acute threat to life can constitute a denial of an appropriate medical screening

375
00:21:03,944 --> 00:21:04,639
examination.

376
00:21:04,939 --> 00:21:07,586
And immediately, the panel fenced its own holding.

377
00:21:07,886 --> 00:21:09,929
Much depends upon circumstances.

378
00:21:10,229 --> 00:21:12,969
The court was not saying that waiting is unlawful.

379
00:21:13,269 --> 00:21:18,061
It was saying that in this case, absent any explanation or mitigating circumstances,

380
00:21:18,061 --> 00:21:22,739
the jury could rationally conclude that the Hospital's inaction here amounted to a

381
00:21:22,739 --> 00:21:24,507
deliberate denial of screening.

382
00:21:24,807 --> 00:21:26,525
Absent any explanation.

383
00:21:26,825 --> 00:21:29,751
And then the sentence the whole case is built on.

384
00:21:30,051 --> 00:21:34,463
EMTALA should be read to proscribe both actual and constructive dumping of patients.

385
00:21:35,063 --> 00:21:36,131
Constructive.

386
00:21:36,431 --> 00:21:41,151
The jury's finding that HSF denied Ms. Gonzalez an appropriate screening examination

387
00:21:41,151 --> 00:21:42,050
is unimpugnable.

388
00:21:42,350 --> 00:21:45,461
On the new-trial motion the court added two observations.

389
00:21:45,761 --> 00:21:50,879
Angel Correa's credibility emerged relatively unscathed from cross-examination, and

390
00:21:50,879 --> 00:21:55,812
we cannot fault the jury either for crediting his recollection or for concluding

391
00:21:55,812 --> 00:22:00,622
that the Hospital denied Ms. Gonzalez any vestige of an appropriate screening.

392
00:22:00,922 --> 00:22:05,174
And on coverage, it noticed a silence: the hospital, which could have supplied a

393
00:22:05,174 --> 00:22:09,479
foolproof answer from its own records, offered nothing to suggest that it did not

394
00:22:09,479 --> 00:22:10,860
welcome Medicare patients.

395
00:22:11,160 --> 00:22:15,164
The jury, the court summarized, heard testimony from which it could have concluded

396
00:22:15,164 --> 00:22:19,071
that Ms. Gonzalez went to the Hospital in critical condition and received only a

397
00:22:19,071 --> 00:22:20,634
high number and a cold shoulder.

398
00:22:20,934 --> 00:22:22,838
A high number and a cold shoulder.

399
00:22:23,138 --> 00:22:24,113
Forty-seven.

400
00:22:24,413 --> 00:22:25,388
Twenty-four.

401
00:22:25,688 --> 00:22:30,032
A number in a hand, an attendant calling a different one, and between them a

402
00:22:30,032 --> 00:22:33,861
distance nobody inside that building ever had to explain to anyone.

403
00:22:34,161 --> 00:22:35,461
One argument was left.

404
00:22:35,761 --> 00:22:40,884
Liability under EMTALA, the hospital said, requires an economic motive — the

405
00:22:40,884 --> 00:22:45,467
suspicion that the patient will be unable adequately to pay her way.

406
00:22:45,767 --> 00:22:50,010
Ms. Gonzalez had insurance that permitted her hospital visit if an emergency

407
00:22:50,010 --> 00:22:50,457
existed.

408
00:22:50,757 --> 00:22:52,847
So no such motive could have existed.

409
00:22:53,147 --> 00:22:58,209
The court reviewed that de novo, as a pure question of law, and rejected it flatly.

410
00:22:58,509 --> 00:23:03,667
Every court of appeals that has considered this issue has concluded that a desire to

411
00:23:03,667 --> 00:23:08,518
shirk the burden of uncompensated care is not a necessary element of a cause of

412
00:23:08,518 --> 00:23:09,747
action under EMTALA.

413
00:23:10,047 --> 00:23:12,137
Then the holding, in its own words.

414
00:23:12,437 --> 00:23:17,508
We hold, therefore, that EMTALA, by its terms, covers all patients who come to a

415
00:23:17,508 --> 00:23:22,706
hospital's emergency department, and requires that they be appropriately screened,

416
00:23:22,706 --> 00:23:25,812
regardless of insurance status or ability to pay.

417
00:23:26,112 --> 00:23:30,152
The panel called the argument an oversimplification, and said so in a footnote.

418
00:23:30,452 --> 00:23:33,006
All insurance plans are not created equal.

419
00:23:33,306 --> 00:23:38,208
Given the bewildering array of coverage conditions, deductibles, reimbursement

420
00:23:38,208 --> 00:23:43,424
rates, and the like, sophisticated but esurient providers have ample provocation to

421
00:23:43,424 --> 00:23:48,703
discriminate not only between insured and uninsured patients but also among patients

422
00:23:48,703 --> 00:23:51,092
who are insured under different plans.

423
00:23:51,392 --> 00:23:52,367
Esurient.

424
00:23:52,667 --> 00:23:53,503
Greedy.

425
00:23:53,803 --> 00:23:58,135
And in the statutory appendix at the back of the opinion is a provision that reads

426
00:23:58,135 --> 00:24:01,094
differently once a card has been checked at one o'clock.

427
00:24:01,394 --> 00:24:06,033
A participating hospital may not delay provision of an appropriate medical screening

428
00:24:06,033 --> 00:24:10,617
examination required under subsection (a) of this section in order to inquire about

429
00:24:10,617 --> 00:24:13,654
the individual's method of payment or insurance status.

430
00:24:13,954 --> 00:24:16,137
The court decided none of this on motive.

431
00:24:16,437 --> 00:24:18,852
Under its own holding, it did not have to.

432
00:24:20,652 --> 00:24:22,277
That is liability.

433
00:24:22,577 --> 00:24:26,617
What is left is money, and the strangest part of the case happens here.

434
00:24:26,917 --> 00:24:30,725
The hospital's best legal argument on appeal was about damages.

435
00:24:31,025 --> 00:24:35,827
Its position was that EMTALA compensates the patient's harm and nothing else — that

436
00:24:35,827 --> 00:24:40,455
seven relatives cannot be paid for their own grief under a federal statute about

437
00:24:40,455 --> 00:24:41,381
emergency rooms.

438
00:24:41,681 --> 00:24:43,306
The court never decided it.

439
00:24:43,606 --> 00:24:44,488
Why not?

440
00:24:44,788 --> 00:24:46,274
Look at the chronology.

441
00:24:46,574 --> 00:24:51,325
The hospital first had the opportunity to assert this defense in its answer to the

442
00:24:51,325 --> 00:24:52,658
complaint, and did not.

443
00:24:52,958 --> 00:24:56,580
It omitted it from its submissions for the initial scheduling conference.

444
00:24:56,880 --> 00:25:00,974
It omitted it from its submissions for the pretrial conference, so the pretrial

445
00:25:00,974 --> 00:25:05,223
order, signed by all counsel and entered by the district court, made no mention of

446
00:25:05,223 --> 00:25:05,379
it.

447
00:25:05,679 --> 00:25:09,998
Its trial brief enumerated three legal issues and this was not among them.

448
00:25:10,298 --> 00:25:14,596
It moved for judgment as a matter of law at the close of the plaintiffs' case

449
00:25:14,596 --> 00:25:19,062
without raising it, renewed that motion at the close of all the evidence without

450
00:25:19,062 --> 00:25:23,081
adding it, and then raised no objection to the verdict forms or the jury

451
00:25:23,081 --> 00:25:26,598
instructions that put the relatives' own suffering to the jury.

452
00:25:26,898 --> 00:25:29,034
This was a waiver, pure and simple.

453
00:25:29,334 --> 00:25:34,311
Based on this somber record of inattention, the panel held, HSF forfeited the theory

454
00:25:34,311 --> 00:25:36,207
of defense that it now espouses.

455
00:25:36,507 --> 00:25:40,640
It gave special weight to the Hospital's boycott of the final pretrial order.

456
00:25:40,940 --> 00:25:45,785
That order is intended to control the subsequent course of the action, and can be

457
00:25:45,785 --> 00:25:48,417
modified only to prevent manifest injustice.

458
00:25:48,717 --> 00:25:52,711
The post-trial motion that finally raised the point got the sentence it deserved.

459
00:25:53,011 --> 00:25:57,906
This motion is a classic example of a litigant locking the barn door long after the

460
00:25:57,906 --> 00:25:58,909
horse has bolted.

461
00:25:59,209 --> 00:26:04,541
A renewed motion under Rule 50(b) is bounded by the movant's earlier Rule 50(a)

462
00:26:04,541 --> 00:26:05,014
motion.

463
00:26:05,314 --> 00:26:10,087
The movant cannot use such a motion as a vehicle to introduce a legal theory not

464
00:26:10,087 --> 00:26:14,741
distinctly articulated in its close-of-evidence motion for a directed verdict.

465
00:26:15,041 --> 00:26:18,013
So can relatives recover under EMTALA for their own anguish?

466
00:26:18,313 --> 00:26:19,520
That was left open.

467
00:26:19,820 --> 00:26:24,345
The court said only that both readings of the statute are superficially plausible,

468
00:26:24,345 --> 00:26:28,926
and that it could not say it was plain error for the lower court, in the absence of

469
00:26:28,926 --> 00:26:32,127
any timely objection, to interpret the statute generously.

470
00:26:32,427 --> 00:26:33,588
Not decided.

471
00:26:33,888 --> 00:26:34,910
Forfeited.

472
00:26:35,210 --> 00:26:37,625
This case turns on that difference twice.

473
00:26:37,925 --> 00:26:41,269
That left excessiveness, reviewed for abuse of discretion.

474
00:26:41,569 --> 00:26:46,416
An award must endure unless it is grossly excessive, inordinate, shocking to the

475
00:26:46,416 --> 00:26:51,384
conscience of the court, or so high that it would be a denial of justice to permit

476
00:26:51,384 --> 00:26:52,111
it to stand.

477
00:26:52,411 --> 00:26:56,451
The evidence behind the five hundred thousand dollars was ordinary and exact.

478
00:26:56,751 --> 00:27:01,653
The testimony indicated that the decedent was a matriarchal figure who functioned as

479
00:27:01,653 --> 00:27:03,345
the hub of the family circle.

480
00:27:03,645 --> 00:27:05,270
Her son Angel lived with her.

481
00:27:05,570 --> 00:27:08,496
Her two daughters, Gloria and Esther, resided nearby.

482
00:27:08,796 --> 00:27:13,376
Her deceased son's four children — who lost their father a mere five months before

483
00:27:13,376 --> 00:27:17,341
their grandmother perished — dwelt in her home for much of their lives.

484
00:27:17,641 --> 00:27:22,231
An expert testified that all three of her children suffered depression in the wake

485
00:27:22,231 --> 00:27:26,541
of their mother's death, and that the four grandchildren experienced sadness,

486
00:27:26,541 --> 00:27:30,180
suffering and the like that would take up to five years to abate.

487
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:36,099
At trial, HSF neither rebutted this testimony in kind nor effectively impeached it.

488
00:27:36,399 --> 00:27:41,186
On appeal it cited a case as authority for a proposition exactly the opposite of

489
00:27:41,186 --> 00:27:45,315
what the case holds, without ellipses to signify what it had cut out.

490
00:27:45,615 --> 00:27:49,516
The court called the argument both disingenuous and unpersuasive.

491
00:27:49,816 --> 00:27:54,376
It is hard to doubt, the opinion says, that the plaintiffs suffered when the woman

492
00:27:54,376 --> 00:27:58,268
described by one witness as the trunk of the family tree was cut down.

493
00:27:58,568 --> 00:28:03,026
The sums awarded, the court said, do not shock our collective conscience.

494
00:28:03,326 --> 00:28:06,391
Then the two hundred thousand awarded on her own account.

495
00:28:06,691 --> 00:28:10,592
Though generous, the jury's assessment does not outstrip the bounds of reason.

496
00:28:10,892 --> 00:28:13,121
What was the two hundred thousand for?

497
00:28:13,421 --> 00:28:15,604
The court gave its reason in two sentences.

498
00:28:15,904 --> 00:28:20,727
Due to the Hospital's failure to provide even the most rudimentary screening, the

499
00:28:20,727 --> 00:28:25,669
court wrote, Ms. Gonzalez spent the few remaining hours of her life in agony, beset

500
00:28:25,669 --> 00:28:27,932
by nausea, dizziness, and chest pains.

501
00:28:28,232 --> 00:28:33,451
It is hard to imagine — let alone to quantify in dollars — the sheer terror that she

502
00:28:33,451 --> 00:28:37,613
must have felt while waiting for medical attention that never came.

503
00:28:37,913 --> 00:28:39,074
Not her death.

504
00:28:39,374 --> 00:28:40,581
The waiting.

505
00:28:40,881 --> 00:28:45,951
This case, the court added, in which the decedent's travails extended over a period

506
00:28:45,951 --> 00:28:51,021
of several hours, is unlike cases involving sudden death in which a decedent's pain

507
00:28:51,021 --> 00:28:55,602
and suffering is limited to a few seconds or, at most, a matter of minutes.

508
00:28:55,902 --> 00:28:57,342
Nothing was reversed.

509
00:28:57,642 --> 00:28:59,082
Nothing was vacated.

510
00:28:59,382 --> 00:29:00,961
No remittitur was ordered.

511
00:29:01,261 --> 00:29:03,351
We need go no further, the panel wrote.

512
00:29:03,651 --> 00:29:08,193
HSF has not presented arguments capable of overcoming the formidable hurdles it

513
00:29:08,193 --> 00:29:12,965
faces in challenging either the liability determination or the damage assessment of

514
00:29:12,965 --> 00:29:14,518
a properly instructed jury.

515
00:29:14,818 --> 00:29:17,465
The judgment below must therefore be affirmed.

516
00:29:19,265 --> 00:29:23,519
What this case gives is narrower than it sounds, and the court was careful to say

517
00:29:23,519 --> 00:29:23,677
so.

518
00:29:23,977 --> 00:29:25,927
It does not guarantee treatment.

519
00:29:26,227 --> 00:29:30,962
EMTALA promises a screening — some procedure, administered even-handedly — and

520
00:29:30,962 --> 00:29:33,936
stabilisation if an emergency condition is found.

521
00:29:34,236 --> 00:29:37,487
Bad care is a malpractice claim, not this one.

522
00:29:37,787 --> 00:29:42,431
An emergency room, the panel said in the middle of its own holding, cannot serve

523
00:29:42,431 --> 00:29:43,824
everyone simultaneously.

524
00:29:44,124 --> 00:29:46,307
It does not make delay unlawful.

525
00:29:46,607 --> 00:29:50,871
The court expressly refused to say whether ordinary negligence in slow-walking a

526
00:29:50,871 --> 00:29:53,109
screening would breach the statute at all.

527
00:29:53,409 --> 00:29:58,738
It held only that this delay, on this record, ran so far past justification that the

528
00:29:58,738 --> 00:30:00,514
screening counted as denied.

529
00:30:00,814 --> 00:30:03,322
It does not find that the hospital dumped her.

530
00:30:03,622 --> 00:30:05,572
A jury found a screening violation.

531
00:30:05,872 --> 00:30:11,165
The court of appeals held that finding unimpugnable, and said, as a proposition of

532
00:30:11,165 --> 00:30:16,135
law, that the statute reaches constructive dumping as well as actual dumping.

533
00:30:16,435 --> 00:30:19,918
Those are two different sentences, and the case is the second one.

534
00:30:20,218 --> 00:30:23,004
It does not hold that the transfer was unlawful.

535
00:30:23,304 --> 00:30:25,487
That finding was never reviewed.

536
00:30:25,787 --> 00:30:30,338
What is left is a method, and it is the most portable thing in the opinion.

537
00:30:30,638 --> 00:30:35,248
To find out whether an emergency room had screened someone, the court did not call a

538
00:30:35,248 --> 00:30:36,071
medical expert.

539
00:30:36,371 --> 00:30:41,083
It asked what the hospital's own rules required, and then asked what the hospital

540
00:30:41,083 --> 00:30:41,897
could produce.

541
00:30:42,197 --> 00:30:43,172
Vital signs.

542
00:30:43,472 --> 00:30:44,772
A written chart.

543
00:30:45,072 --> 00:30:47,394
A referral to an in-house physician.

544
00:30:47,694 --> 00:30:52,089
Four rules the hospital wrote for itself, and nothing at all produced for the woman

545
00:30:52,089 --> 00:30:53,360
who sat there two hours.

546
00:30:53,660 --> 00:30:56,028
There is no villain in this record.

547
00:30:56,328 --> 00:30:58,511
No decision was ever testified to.

548
00:30:58,811 --> 00:31:03,362
Nobody was shown to have looked at an insurance card and formed a judgment about the

549
00:31:03,362 --> 00:31:04,337
person holding it.

550
00:31:04,637 --> 00:31:06,820
That is what the court's word is for.

551
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:08,931
Constructive.

552
00:31:09,231 --> 00:31:11,971
It does not require anybody to decide anything.

553
00:31:12,271 --> 00:31:16,607
Somewhere in that building, while she was still sitting in it, an attendant called

554
00:31:16,607 --> 00:31:17,612
number twenty-four.

555
00:31:17,912 --> 00:31:19,305
Nothing was refused.

556
00:31:19,605 --> 00:31:20,859
Nothing was said.

557
00:31:21,459 --> 00:31:23,224
Forty-seven was never called.
